## 15.1 to 15.5 Omitted

## SECTION II – PROVISIONS ABOUT REGISTERED TRADE MARKS AND OTHER INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY RIGHTS

## Allocation (rule 63.13)

## 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (1) copyright;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (2) rights in performances;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (3) rights conferred under Part VII of the 1988 Act;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (4) design right conferred under Part III of the 1988 Act;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (5) Unregistered Community design right;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (5A) supplementary unregistered design right;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (6) association rights, including the right of the Birmingham Organising Committee by virtue of section 3(4) of the Birmingham Commonwealth Games Act 2020 to take action for breach of section 3(1) of that Act (prohibition of unauthorised association with the Games);

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (7) moral rights;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (8) database rights;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (9) unauthorised decryption rights;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (10) hallmarks;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (11) claims in respect of technical trade secrets;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (12) passing off;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (13) protected designations of origin, protected geographical indications and traditional speciality guarantees;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] (14) registered trade marks

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] 16.2 There are Chancery district registries at Birmingham, Bristol, Caernarfon, Cardiff, Leeds, Liverpool, Manchester, Mold, Newcastle upon Tyne and Preston. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 16.1 The other intellectual property rights referred to in rule 63.13 are –] 16.3 The County Court hearing centres at Caernarfon, Mold and Preston do not have jurisdiction in relation to registered trade marks. ## Starting the claim

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Starting the claim] 17.1 Except for claims started in the Intellectual Property Enterprise Court, a claim form to which Section II of Part 63 applies must be marked in the top right hand corner ‘Intellectual Property’ below the title of the court in which it is issued. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Starting the claim] 17.2 In the case of claims concerning registered trade marks, the claim form must state the registration number of any trade mark to which the claim relates. ## Reference to the court by the registrar or the Comptroller

## 18.1 This paragraph applies where –

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 18.1 This paragraph applies where –] (1) an application is made to the registrar under the 1994 Act and the registrar refers the application to the court; or

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 18.1 This paragraph applies where –] (2) a reference is made to the Comptroller under section 246 of the 1988 Act and the Comptroller refers the whole proceedings or a particular question or issue to the court under section 251(1) of that Act. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 18.1 This paragraph applies where –] 18.2 Where paragraph 18.1 applies, the applicant under the 1994 Act or the person making the reference under section 246 of the 1988 Act, as the case may be, must start a claim seeking the court’s determination of the reference within 14 days of receiving notification of the decision to refer. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 18.1 This paragraph applies where –] 18.3 If the person referred to in paragraph 18.2 does not start a claim within the period prescribed by that paragraph, that person will be deemed to have abandoned the reference. ## 18.4 The period prescribed under paragraph 18.2 may be extended by –

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 18.4 The period prescribed under paragraph 18.2 may be extended by –] (1) the registrar or the Comptroller as the case may be; or

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 18.4 The period prescribed under paragraph 18.2 may be extended by –] (2) the court

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 18.4 The period prescribed under paragraph 18.2 may be extended by –] where a party so applies, even if the application is not made until after the expiration of that period. ## Application to the court under section 19 of the 1994 Act

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Application to the court under section 19 of the 1994 Act] 19.1 Where an application is made under section 19 of the 1994 Act, the applicant must serve the claim form or application notice on all identifiable persons having an interest in the goods, materials or articles within the meaning of section 19 of the 1994 Act. ## Order affecting entry in the register of trade marks

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Order affecting entry in the register of trade marks] 20.1 Where any order of the court affects the validity of an entry in the register, the provisions of paragraphs 14.1 and 14.2 apply. ## 21.1 to 21.5 Omitted

## Claim for additional damages under section 97(2), section 191J(2) or section 229(3) of the 1988 Act

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Claim for additional damages under section 97(2), section 191J(2) or section 229(3) of the 1988 Act] 22.1 Where a claimant seeks to recover additional damages under section 97(2), section 191J(2) or section 229(3) of the 1988 Act, the particulars of claim must include –

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Claim for additional damages under section 97(2), section 191J(2) or section 229(3) of the 1988 Act] (1) a statement to that effect; and

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Claim for additional damages under section 97(2), section 191J(2) or section 229(3) of the 1988 Act] (2) the grounds for claiming them. ## Application for delivery up or forfeiture under the 1988 Act

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Application for delivery up or forfeiture under the 1988 Act] 23.1 An applicant who applies under section 99, 114, 195, 204, 230 or 231 of the 1988 Act for delivery up or forfeiture must serve–

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Application for delivery up or forfeiture under the 1988 Act] (1) the claim form; or

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Application for delivery up or forfeiture under the 1988 Act] (2) application notice, where appropriate,

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Application for delivery up or forfeiture under the 1988 Act] on all identifiable persons who have an interest in the goods, material or articles within the meaning of section 114, 204 or 231 of the 1988 Act. ## Association rights

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Association rights] 24.1 Where an application is made under regulations made under section 7 of the Olympic Symbol etc (Protection) Act 1995, the applicant must serve the claim form or application notice on all identifiable persons having an interest in the goods, materials or articles within the meaning of the regulations. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Association rights] 24.2 Where the Birmingham Organising Committee applies for an order under section 6 of the Birmingham Commonwealth Games Act 2020, a copy of the application must be served on all identifiable persons having an interest in the infringing goods or documents. ## SECTION III – PROVISIONS ABOUT APPEALS

## Reference to the court by an appointed person

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Reference to the court by an appointed person] 25.1 This paragraph applies where a person appointed by the Lord Chancellor to hear and decide appeals under section 77 of the 1994 Act, refers an appeal to the Chancery Division under section 76(3) of the 1994 Act. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Reference to the court by an appointed person] 25.2 The appellant must file a claim form seeking the court’s determination of the appeal within 14 days of receiving notification of the decision to refer. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Reference to the court by an appointed person] 25.3 The appeal will be deemed to have been abandoned if the appellant does not file a claim form within the period prescribed by paragraph 25.2. ## 25.4 The period prescribed under paragraph 25.2 may be extended by –

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 25.4 The period prescribed under paragraph 25.2 may be extended by –] (1) the person appointed by the Lord Chancellor; or

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 25.4 The period prescribed under paragraph 25.2 may be extended by –] (2) the court

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 25.4 The period prescribed under paragraph 25.2 may be extended by –] where the appellant so applies, even if such application is not made until after the expiration of that period. ## SECTION IV – PROVISIONS ABOUT FINAL ORDERS

## Costs

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Costs] 26.1 Where the court makes an order for delivery up or destruction of infringing goods, or articles designed or adapted to make such goods, the person against whom the order is made must pay the costs of complying with that order unless the court orders otherwise. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Costs] 26.2 Where the court finds that an intellectual property right has been infringed, the court may, at the request of the applicant, order appropriate measures for the dissemination and publication of the judgment to be taken at the expense of the infringer. ## SECTION V – PROVISIONS ABOUT PROCEEDINGS IN THE INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY ENTERPRISE COURT

## Scope of Section V

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Scope of Section V] 27.1 Except as provided for in paragraph 27.2 this Practice Direction, as modified by this Section, applies to claims in the Intellectual Property Enterprise Court. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Scope of Section V] 27.2 Paragraph 5.2, paragraphs 5.10 to 9.1 and paragraph 9.2(3) do not apply to a claim in the Intellectual Property Enterprise Court. ## Claims for infringement or challenge to validity

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Claims for infringement or challenge to validity] 28.1 Paragraph 4.2(2) is modified so that the grounds for invalidity must be included in the statement of case and not in a separate document. ## Case management (rule 63.23)

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Case management (rule 63.23)] 29.1 At the case management conference referred to in rule 63.23 the court may order any of the following –

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Case management (rule 63.23)] (1) specific disclosure;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Case management (rule 63.23)] (2) a product or process description (or a supplementary product or process description where one has already been provided);

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Case management (rule 63.23)] (3) experiments;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Case management (rule 63.23)] (4) witness statements;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Case management (rule 63.23)] (5) experts’ reports;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Case management (rule 63.23)] (6) cross examination at trial;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Case management (rule 63.23)] (7) written submissions or skeleton arguments. ## 29.2 The court will make an order under paragraph 29.1 only –

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 29.2 The court will make an order under paragraph 29.1 only –] (1) in relation to specific and identified issues; and

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 29.2 The court will make an order under paragraph 29.1 only –] (2) if the court is satisfied that the benefit of the further material in terms of its value in resolving those issues appears likely to justify the cost of producing and dealing with it. ## Applications (rule 63.25)

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Applications (rule 63.25)] 30.1 Where the court considers that a hearing is necessary under rule 63.25(3) the court will conduct a hearing by telephone or video conference in accordance with paragraphs 6.2 to 7 of Practice Direction 23A unless it considers that a hearing in person would be more cost effective for the parties or is otherwise necessary in the interests of justice. ## Determination of the claim

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Determination of the claim] 31.1 Where possible, the court will determine the claim solely on the basis of the parties’ statements of case and oral submissions. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Determination of the claim] 31.2 The court will set the timetable for the trial and will, so far as appropriate, allocate equal time to the parties. Cross-examination will be strictly controlled by the court. The court will endeavour to ensure that the trial lasts no more than 2 days. ## Small claims

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > Small claims] 32.1 This Practice Direction shall apply to a claim allocated to the small claims track in the Intellectual Property Enterprise Court to the extent provided by paragraphs 32.2 and 32.3. ## 32.2 The following paragraphs shall apply –

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 32.2 The following paragraphs shall apply –] (1) in Section II, 16.1, 17.1, 17.2, 19.1, 20.1, 21.1 to 21.5, 22.1, 23.1, 24.1;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 32.2 The following paragraphs shall apply –] (2) in Section IV, 26.1, 26.2;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 63A – INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY CLAIMS > 32.2 The following paragraphs shall apply –] (3) in Section V, 30.1, 31.1. ## 32.3 No other provision in this Practice Direction shall apply.
